        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Atlantic meridional overturning circulation",
            "source_entity_type": "Ocean_Circulation",
            "relation": "OBSERVED_IN_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "North Atlantic",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "A common explanation for these transitions involves changes in the Atlantic meridional overturning circulation (AMOC), perhaps controlled by freshwater perturbation (for example, refs 4,5) and/or Northern Hemisphere ice sheet changes (for example, refs 6-8). To reproduce the abrupt transitions into and out of cold conditions across the North Atlantic (that is, AMOC weak or 'off mode'), a common trigger mechanism is related to the timing of North Atlantic freshwater perturbations.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Heinrich events",
            "source_entity_type": "Weather_Event",
            "relation": "IMPACTED_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "North Atlantic",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "The most pronounced stadial coolings, Heinrich events, are associated with massive iceberg discharges to the North Atlantic.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "COSMOS",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "HAS_SIMULATION_TYPE",
            "target_entity_instance": "coupled",
            "target_entity_type": "SimulationType",
            "paragraph": "Here we show that, in a fully coupled atmosphere-ocean model, gradual changes in atmospheric CO2 concentrations can trigger abrupt climate changes, associated with a regime of bi-stability of the Atlantic meridional overturning circulation under intermediate glacial conditions.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": false,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "North Atlantic Deep Water",
            "source_entity_type": "Ocean_Circulation",
            "relation": "OBSERVED_IN_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "North Atlantic",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "A key mechanism responsible for changes in the subtropical sea surface salinity is the zonal atmospheric moisture transport across Central America. Previous data and model studies suggest that a southward shift of the intertropical convergence zone (ITCZ) is responsible for the salinity increase in the western subtropical North Atlantic (WSNA, 60\u00b0\u201390\u00b0 W, 10\u00b0\u201330\u00b0 N) during cold stadial periods. This is presumed to be a precondition for North Atlantic Deep Water (NADW) formation to abruptly return to warm interstadial conditions with a strong AMOC mode.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "North Atlantic Subpolar Gyre",
            "source_entity_type": "Ocean_Circulation",
            "relation": "OBSERVED_IN_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "North Atlantic",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "As a consequence, the warmer SAT enhances the local cyclonic wind stress that strengthens the North Atlantic Subpolar Gyre (Fig. 2e and Supplementary Fig. 2a,e). This in turn strengthens local sea-ice variability, shifting its probability distribution from a single peak to a double peak prior to the AMOC resumption (Supplementary Fig. 10).",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        }
    ],
